= Cache memory =
<onlyinclude>'''Cache memory''' is a hardware structure that keeps copies of recently or predictively useful memory blocks near a processor or other requester. It reduces the cost of some accesses by exploiting locality, but its performance depends on address mapping, replacement, write behavior, and interaction with other caches.</onlyinclude>

== Scope ==
A cache stores fixed-size '''lines''' with identifying tags and validity state. A hit finds a requested line; a miss requires access to another level or backing memory. Caches may be instruction, data, unified, private or shared. They differ from the larger topic of [[Computer memory]]: this article focuses on how hardware caches are organized, evaluated and kept coherent.

== Design questions ==
* '''Placement:''' direct mapping, set associativity, and full associativity determine where a line may reside.
* '''Replacement:''' policies such as least recently used (LRU), approximations, or random selection choose victims when a set is full.
* '''Writes:''' write-through and write-back differ in when modified data reaches the next level; write-allocate and no-write-allocate differ on write misses.
* '''Hierarchy:''' inclusion, exclusion and noninclusive policies govern relationships among levels, with implementation-dependent exceptions.
* '''Sharing:''' coherence protocols coordinate copies of writable data among agents. Coherence is not the same as a complete memory-consistency model.

== Evidence and limits ==
Cache hit rates and access times are workload- and implementation-dependent. Higher associativity may reduce conflict misses but can increase area, energy and access latency. A cache does not guarantee that a given program will run faster.

